%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Binary Detector Confidence}
\label{sec:binary_confidence}

Binary detectors (DivEye~\cite{basani2026diveye}, Binoculars~\cite{hans2024binoculars}) show moderate rank correlations with depth ($|\rho| = 0.06$--$0.46$; Table~\ref{tab:s9_binary_spearman}) but fail as depth proxies: 6-class accuracy is at or below chance ($\leq 0.199$ vs.\ 0.167 baseline), pairwise $\auc$ yields $\Kempirical^{\text{binary}} \leq 1$, and multiple conditions exhibit non-monotonic AUC at deeper pairs.
The observed correlation is an artifact of 0v1 separation rather than fine-grained depth signal.

\section{Application: $\Kstar$-Aware Data Filtering}
\label{sec:filtering}

$\Kempirical = 2$ makes a falsifiable prediction: binary curation should suffice, and graduated performance should converge toward binary as the decay steepness increases.
We compare six strategies on 30{,}000 documents (depths 0--5): (i)~\textbf{no-filter}, (ii)~\textbf{binary-ours} ($w = 1 - P(\text{synthetic})$ from 15D LightGBM), (iii)~\textbf{binary-Drayson}~\citep{drayson2025}, (iv)~\textbf{graduated} ($w = \exp(-\alpha \cdot \hat{d})$, $\alpha \in \{0.1,\ldots,0.9\}$), (v)~\textbf{surprisal} (retain above median), and (vi)~\textbf{DSIR}~\citep{xie2023dsir}.
We fine-tune Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct via LoRA (rank~8, $\alpha_{\text{LoRA}}{=}16$) for 3 epochs on MMLU and HellaSwag across 5 seeds (51 total runs: 5 base strategies $\times$ 5 seeds $+$ 5 graduated-$\alpha$ values $\times$ 5 seeds $+$ 1 base-model reference; this 100\% synthetic setting is an upper bound~\cite{shumailov2024collapse, dohmatob2024model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Downstream task performance (MMLU, HellaSwag) under six data-filtering strategies. Binary-ours uses the 15D LightGBM depth classifier; Graduated applies exponentially decaying weights $w = \exp(-\alpha \hat{d})$. Mean $\pm$ std over 5 seeds. Full $\alpha$ sweep in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.}
\label{tab:tab_filtering_main}
\input{tables/tab_filtering_main}
\end{table}

Binary-ours minimizes MMLU degradation most effectively (Table~\ref{tab:tab_filtering_main}), reducing the gap from $-$1.53\,pp (no-filter) to $-$0.77\,pp (${\sim}$50\% reduction; $p_{\text{corr}} < 0.05$ vs.\ all alternatives).
These effects are measured in a 100\% synthetic upper-bound setting; real-world contamination ratios are typically lower, and effect sizes would scale accordingly.
The strategy ordering is interpretable through $\Kempirical$: binary filtering exploits the one reliable boundary (0v1), while graduated strategies assign weights to depths 2--5 based on near-random classifier outputs, injecting noise.
Graduated filtering converges monotonically toward binary: at $\alpha{=}0.9$, it matches binary-ours on HellaSwag exactly ($\Delta = 0$, $p_{\text{corr}} = 1.00$) but remains significantly worse on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} = 0.011$).
DSIR is indistinguishable from no-filter, confirming that depth-aware features are necessary for curation in recursively contaminated corpora (Figure~\ref{fig:filtering_strategies} in Appendix).
Binary filtering retains a consistent advantage across contamination levels (10--50\%), with effect sizes diminishing 3--6$\times$ relative to the 100\% setting; graduated weighting remains indistinguishable from no-filter at all tested ratios, confirming that depth assignments beyond $\Kempirical$ inject noise regardless of severity (Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response}).

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}
\label{sec:boundary_cases}
\label{sec:length_confound}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jsd_decay.pdf}
\caption{Token-level JSD between adjacent depth pairs on log scale. Blue band: min-max range across 7 C4 models with $\Kempirical{=}2$; dashed purple: Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$, cross-scorer validated); green: arXiv Pythia ($\Kempirical{=}3$); dotted red: retrain-chain ($\Kempirical{\geq}4$). C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$), while arXiv decays gradually ($\lambda \approx 1.0$) and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$).}
\label{fig:jsd_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ reflects concentrated information loss: 0v1 is near-trivially separable ($\auc \geq 0.92$), 1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp, and 2v3 falls below $\theta$.
A single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from multiply recycled content, limiting depth-aware curation to a binary boundary.
Converging evidence supports a data-intrinsic interpretation: OBD achieves 96\% depth-0 recall but only 14--22\% for depths 2--4; frontier LLMs show 8--38\% (McNemar $p > 0.05$ vs.\ OBD; Table~\ref{tab:s3_llm_zeroshot}).

\paragraph{JSD decay and the discrimination boundary.}
The token-level JSD between adjacent generations reveals two distinct decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}).
For C4 web text, 0v1 JSD exceeds 1v2 by 6--56$\times$ across all 7 models with $\Kempirical{=}2$ (median cliff ratio ${\sim}$15$\times$), and subsequent pairs converge to a noise floor of ${\sim}$0.003--0.005.
Fitting $\jsd(k, k{+}1) \propto e^{-\lambda k}$ yields $\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$ for C4 (mean ${\approx}\,2.9$), consistent with rapid mixing of $G_\delta$ toward its stationary distribution.
These decay rates represent qualitative characterizations from 5-point curves rather than precise estimates.
The empirical decay rate $\lambda$ is consistent within each domain but varies across domains: C4 yields $\lambda \approx 2.9$, Wikipedia $\lambda \approx 3.1$ (excluding OLMo, whose $\lambda = 6.3$ reflects a capacity-limited collapse: 0v1 $\jsd = 0.169$ with near-complete information loss at the first step, which makes the exponential fit unreliable), and arXiv $\lambda \approx 1.0$.
This cross-model consistency indicates that the contraction rate of $G_\delta$ is governed primarily by the structure of the input domain rather than the model architecture.
ArXiv text decays ${\sim}$3$\times$ slower than C4 ($\lambda \approx 1.0$ vs.\ $2.9$), with a correspondingly higher convergence floor (${\sim}$0.007--0.009 vs.\ ${\sim}$0.003--0.004).
Formulaic structural features (LaTeX formatting, citation patterns, section templates) degrade more slowly under autoregressive generation and supply persistent depth-distinguishing signal, which explains the elevated $\Kempirical{=}3$.
The retrain-chain exhibits a fundamentally different profile: $\lambda = 0.285$ with a cliff ratio of only 1.31$\times$ and no sharp drop between adjacent pairs.
This gradual, sustained divergence reflects cumulative model-adaptation artifacts rather than text-intrinsic information loss, further supporting the distinction between prompt-chain and retrain-chain $\Kempirical$ (Section~\ref{sec:boundary_cases}).

\paragraph{Graduated filtering failure.}
For web text, 2v3 $\auc < \theta$ across all model configurations, so depth assignments for $d \geq 2$ approximate random labels; graduated weighting injects noise, explaining convergence toward binary at high $\alpha$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).
DSIR fails differently: bigram hashing captures domain shift but not depth-correlated signatures.
This pattern persists across contamination ratios (10--50\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response}), indicating the failure is structural rather than severity-dependent.

\paragraph{Representation dependence.}
$\Kempirical$ is representation-dependent: frozen Qwen embeddings yield $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$, while Pythia and OLMo match the 15D boundary at $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$ (Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}).
The 15D features trade one depth level of sensitivity for cross-model consistency (7/8 configurations $\Kempirical{\leq}2$), making them the preferred operational choice.
Cross-scorer validation is consistent with $\Kempirical$ being governed by the generation operator rather than the scoring model ($\rho > 0.90$ feature ranking preservation; length confound ruled out in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}), though $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}{=}3$ for Qwen indicates that representation choice can modulate the boundary.

\paragraph{Domain dependence of $\Kempirical$.}
C4 web text consistently yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (7/8 model configurations), while structured academic text (arXiv) yields $\Kempirical{=}3$ across five models spanning four architecture families (Section~\ref{sec:cross_domain}).
Domain-specific stylistic constraints amplify per-generation distributional shift, making contamination in structured corpora trackable to greater depth.
With scorer confound controlled (same-family Qwen scoring inflates $\Kempirical$ by +1 on Wikipedia), all six models yield $\Kempirical{=}1$ on Wikipedia (Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}), producing a uniform domain ordering: arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) $>$ C4 ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $>$ Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$).
Source-domain structural complexity is the sole factor that systematically shifts $\Kempirical$ across all tested model configurations.

\paragraph{Prompt-chain vs.\ retrain-chain $\Kempirical$.}
Retrain-chain experiments (Pythia-1.4B LoRA) yield $\Kempirical{\geq}4$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.9484$, 2v3 $= 0.8888$, 3v4 $= 0.7982$; bootstrap CIs in Table~\ref{tab:retrain_ci}), far exceeding the prompt-chain boundary of $\Kempirical{=}2$.
The elevated retrain-chain signal arises from cumulative model-adaptation artifacts (vocabulary narrowing, repetition bias) that inject additional discriminative signal beyond text-intrinsic information loss.
These two boundaries address distinct operational questions.
Prompt-chain $\Kempirical{=}2$ measures text-level information decay: after one generation pass, the text itself carries insufficient signal to distinguish further recycling, regardless of how many times it has been regenerated.
This is the relevant boundary for data curation on wild web text, where one cannot assume that synthetic documents originate from a retrain pipeline.
Retrain-chain $\Kempirical{\geq}4$ reflects model-adaptation artifacts that accumulate across fine-tuning iterations (setup-specific vocabulary narrowing, increased repetition); these artifacts are detectable but do not imply proportionally greater text quality degradation.
The two protocols are complementary rather than contradictory: prompt-chain $\Kempirical$ provides the operational lower bound for corpus filtering, while retrain-chain $\Kempirical$ characterizes model-level drift in closed-loop training scenarios.
This retrain-chain result is based on a single model (Pythia-1.4B); cross-model generalization remains to be verified.

\paragraph{Boundary cases and scale effect.}
Of 8 tested model configurations on C4, 7 yield $\Kempirical{=}2$; Mistral-7B is the sole sub-2 exception ($\Kempirical{=}1$; 1v2 $\auc = 0.594$, CI $[0.584, 0.603]$), validating $\theta{=}0.60$ as an effective discriminator (gap: $0.594$ vs.\ $0.611$--$0.696$).
Mistral's cross-domain profile reinforces the domain-governed interpretation: despite $\Kempirical{=}1$ on both C4 and Wikipedia, it yields $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv (1v2 $\auc = 0.820$, 2v3 $= 0.628$), and this profile persists under cross-scorer validation (Pythia-1.4B scorer: C4 $\Kempirical{=}1$, arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ (2v3 $\auc = 0.608$, CI $[0.593, 0.622]$)), indicating that domain structural complexity can rescue even web-text one-wash generators (Table~\ref{tab:domain_kstar}).
Larger models produce more human-like text, reducing 0v1 JSD: 0.16--0.29 at $\leq$1.5B, 0.077--0.13 at 7--8B (LLaMA-3.1-8B, Qwen-7B, Mistral-7B).
This trend suggests that models at 13B+ may increasingly yield $\Kempirical{=}1$; this speculative prediction warrants future verification.

\paragraph{Scorer sensitivity.}
$\Kempirical$ can depend on scorer family: on Wikipedia, Qwen generators scored by Qwen-1.5B yield $\Kempirical{=}2$, but cross-family scoring with Pythia-1.4B reduces this to $\Kempirical{=}1$ ($\Delta = -2.4$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}).
On C4, cross-scorer validation preserves feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$) and $\Kempirical$ for 6/8 configurations (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).
ArXiv is the most robust domain under cross-scoring: 3/4 generators preserve $\Kempirical{=}3$ with Pythia-1.4B scorer, compared to 0/2 Qwen generators retaining $\Kempirical{=}2$ on Wikipedia.
Scorer sensitivity is thus domain-dependent rather than pervasive, and inversely related to domain structural complexity.
Structured domains such as arXiv supply redundant distributional cues (LaTeX formatting, citation patterns, mathematical notation) that survive scorer substitution, whereas simpler domains rely on a narrower signal channel that scorer family bias can mask.
We recommend cross-family scorer validation as a standard robustness check for $\Kempirical$ claims on new domains.

\section{Extended Discussion}
\label{sec:extended_discussion}

\subsection{Theoretical Implications of $\Kempirical = 2$}

The DPI guarantees a finite $\Ktheory$; for general web text, $\Kempirical \leq 2$ across all 8 configurations (1B--8B, 5 families), while structured domains (arXiv) yield $\Kempirical{=}3$, aligning with model collapse convergence~\cite{shumailov2024collapse, dohmatob2024model, alemohammad2024selfconsuming}.
Decoding strategy modestly affects signal strength (1v2 $\auc \in [0.6866, 0.7039]$) but does not shift the boundary.

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer Implications}

Cross-model classification retains 80--92\% accuracy with scorer-invariant feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$), enabling single-classifier deployment across generators.
Absolute discriminability at the $\Kempirical$ boundary is scorer-sensitive: alternative scorers reduce Qwen-continuation $\Kempirical$ from 2 to 1, while rewrite-mode $\Kempirical = 2$ is preserved.
Model family similarity modulates transfer (Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo: 90--92\% vs.\ Qwen-cont$\leftrightarrow$OLMo: 80--85\%).

